RR: My mother was born in Riga, Latvia.

INT: When did she leave and why?

RR: Same thing. I don't know.

INT: What work did she do?

RR: My mother was a housewife.

INT: What do you know about relationships between the Jews and their neighbors in Amsterdam before the war?

RR: I know nothing about Amsterdam before the war.

INT: OK. What religious traditions did your family observe?

RR: My parents were Orthodox Jewish.

INT: Do you remember anything from your childhood, any religious observances?

RR: Well, I do remember my father taking me to synagogue on Shabbat. I also remember going to Seder services during Passover.

INT: Were you an only child, or did you have siblings?

RR: I had a brother.

INT: Older or younger?

RR: My brother was two years older.

INT: And his name?

RR: My brother's name was Henry.

INT: What are some of your earliest memories?

RR: Oh, my earliest memories were living in Antwerp, and going to school there, and going with my parents on Sundays to the park. That about sums it up about my really early memories.

INT: Oh. Let me rephrase my question from before. I think I asked you about Amsterdam instead of Antwerp. I apologize. Can you tell me what the relationships were between Jews and Christians in your hometown?

RR: I really can't answer that. I was only eight or nine years old. I had friends who were Jewish, and I had friends who were Gentiles. But I really can't answer that question.

INT: You have no memories of antisemitism in your childhood.

RR: No, not in Belgium.

INT: I know you were young, but how much do you think your parents knew about the Nazis before the war came to you? Were they alarmed by what was going on?

RR: Oh, yes. My parents, as I learned later, were very much aware, mostly because I knew about the planning that they had made for us to try to leave Belgium.

INT: What do you remember about those plans?

RR: Well, I remember about what happened when we ended up both in Dunkirk and in Barcelona, Spain. All of that was due to my parents' planning.

INT: Can you take me by the hand and walk me through first Dunkirk and then we can go beyond that?

RR: Yes.

INT: So let's first talk--

RR: [CLEARS THROAT] Well, the war started in September of 1939 in the spring of 1940, Belgium was already involved in the war, although Belgium was not occupied by the Germans at that time. And my parents decided that it was time to leave. My father had a business in London, England, so plans were made to go to the England. And we traveled first to Boulogne and then to Calais, which was very close to Dunkirk. Then we arrived in Dunkirk. And unfortunately, we were caught in no-man's land between a little over a quarter million British soldiers trying to get over to England and the Germans basically coming closer and attacking. And we were right in the middle. Eventually, the Germans caught up to us, and my parents did speak German. And they escorted us back into the German lines and then back to Antwerp, Belgium, where we stayed for another year. During that period, my father was arrested once. But he was able to bribe his way out of jail and then made plans for us to escape to go to Barcelona, Spain, which we did.

INT: OK. Before you get to Spain, let me just get a little more information about what you just said. How did you travel when you were going to-- you ended up in the area around Dunkirk? How did you travel there?

RR: We traveled by train to Boulogne, and then by bus, I think it was, to Calais. And from there on, it was primarily on foot. Calais was fairly close to Dunkirk.

INT: Was there a specific catalyst that made your parents leave at that time, or was this just--

RR: They--

INT: --they were planning it?

RR: My parents saw the writing on the wall about what the Germans were doing.

INT: And when was this exactly that you ended up in the area around Dunkirk? Can you give me a time?

RR: It was in June of 1940.

INT: Did anybody travel with your family, or was it just you?

RR: No. No, we were by ourselves.

INT: What do you remember of that situation being between the British and the Germans? What memories do you have?

RR: I remember basically, we were all lying on the ground. As a matter of fact, my leg was grazed by a bullet. It was not a big deal, but it did require some care later on when we back-- went back to the German lines. But all I remember was seeing a lot of fighting, a lot of planes shooting at each other, and chaos, basically.

INT: So you had to go back home. And you mentioned your father had been arrested. Do you know why he was arrested?

RR: I don't know for sure. All I can do is guess that he was Jewish.

INT: What memories do you have of that year before you were able to get out again?

RR: OK. Well, as a young boy, I was having a good time. [LAUGHING] After the air raids, we would go outside and try to pick up shrapnels, which was something that we all collected. So I don't remember too much negative, because, as I said, I was only eight, nine years old, and I was having a good time. In those days, the war didn't really mean that much to me.

INT: Did you have to wear a yellow star?

RR: No, there was nothing. There was none of that during the time we were in Antwerp.

INT: Did you witness any brutality at the hands of the Nazis or--

RR: No, we did not.

INT: What was that year like for your parents?

RR: Well, I really can't-- don't have a good answer for that. All I know from later on is that they were very concerned and that they were making plans. Really, my father made plans for us to get on the-- it was a cruise ship leaving Barcelona to eventually end up in Cuba. I knew they were doing that, but at the time, I really wasn't aware of it.

INT: So your second attempt to leave, how did you travel? Can you take me by the hand and walk me through it?

RR: Well, all I can really remember is that we took a train, and we traveled through France. And I think we stopped in Rouen, and then we ended up in the Pyrenees Mountains in a town called Pau, P-A-U. And from there, we ended up literally walking across into Spain and picking up a train there that took us to Barcelona.

INT: When you left, [PAUSES] and you can answer this for either the first attempt to leave or the second attempt, what could you take with you, you the child?

RR: We took very, very little with us. We did not even have large suitcases. [PAUSES] My parents, I think, had some valuables that they were able to take with them, things like diamonds and things of that nature, but very little. We did not have a lot of luggage.

INT: Would you say your family was fairly affluent--

RR: Yes.

INT: --before the war?

RR: Yes, my parents were affluent.

INT: OK, so you came into Spain. How were you treated when you got there?

RR: We were treated very nicely. We lived in a hotel. I think we were there maybe for three, four weeks. I remember sneaking off and going to a bullfight, which really thrilled me in those days, because I was, again, a young boy. And I'd heard about those things but never witnessed one. And then we picked up a ship called the Marques de Comillas, which was a cruise line, which basically took us around through Gibraltar and into a place called Vigo, which was in Spain on the Atlantic side, and then into Cuba.

INT: Before we leave Europe, do you have any other memories that you want to share about your time in Europe?

RR: None. Not really, not any more than what I've already discussed here. I know it's very difficult, because I was-- again, I was a young boy. And I was having a good time.

INT: You were never afraid?

RR: No. It was really-- you know, except at Dunkirk, there was a lot of shooting. But other than that, there was never any real reason. Because my parents sheltered us pretty well, you know, from the harshness of being in occupied Antwerp during those days.

INT: So you left Spain. Can you tell us about the ship? Who were some of the other passengers?

RR: I really don't know too much about it, to tell you the truth. Again, I was having the time of my life. I would go up to the bow of the ship and let the breeze flow over me. And I do remember all that. I did not get seasick, and we had a great cruise into Havana.

INT: You were not aware if other people were refugees also?

RR: No.

INT: You had--

RR: No.

INT: Can you give me a time frame when you left Spain, when you arrived in Cuba?

RR: We left Spain approximately-- it was sometime in the early summer of 1941.

INT: Was your family-- were your parents aware that Jewish refugees had been refused entry to Cuba in 1939 on the St. Louis?

RR: I don't know that. I have no answer for that. Obviously, I became aware of it many, many years later, but--

INT: How were you treated in Cuba?

RR: It was very nice. Obviously, I knew no Spanish, so I had to learn very quickly. I went to school there. We also attended services on Saturdays. Unfortunately, my father ended up having a stroke while we were in Cuba, and that didn't make our stay there much easier. But we did manage.

INT: Did you experience or witness any antisemitism in Cuba?

RR: Not that I was aware of. I had no problems. As I said, you know, we lived in a suburb of Havana called Vedado. And we really, [PAUSES] as far as I know, had no antisemitic-type situations at the time.

INT: How did your family support itself when they were-- when you were in Cuba?

RR: My parents had brought some valuables out from Antwerp. I think they had diamonds, which they were able to sell.

INT: How long were you in Cuba? Can you give me--

RR: We were in Cuba about 11 months. I remember very vividly Pearl Harbor Day. I had gone to the store to pick up some things for my parents, and everybody was talking about it. And by then, I knew enough Spanish to understand what they were saying. Although again, I didn't understand the ramifications, because I knew nothing about Pearl Harbor. But I did know that America was attacked.

INT: Did the fact that America was now in the war have any impact on your entry to this country?

RR: I'm sorry, I couldn't hear you.

INT: Did the fact that the US now was at war after Pearl Harbor-- did that have any impact on you trying to get into the United States?

RR: I think at the latest a bit, because I do remember being ready to leave sometime in the early winter and then being told now we were going to stay a little bit longer. We ended up finally leaving sometime in May or June, I think it was, of 1942.

INT: Was it always your parents' intention to go to the United States?

RR: As far as I know, yes. Originally, it wasn't. Originally, they were going to go to England. But the situation in Dunkirk wiped all that out. And then my parents decided that the United States was really a better situation for us.

INT: How did you leave Cuba, and where did you go?

RR: We flew into Miami. My parents-- one of my parents had a distant relative who was a physician with the immigration service there. And he kind of smoothed things over for us.

INT: Did any of the Jewish agencies help you come to the United States, or was it just privately done?

RR: It was all privately done.

INT: And you were not at all concerned about any quotas coming into the United States?

RR: As far as I knew, no. But again, [LAUGHING] remember, I was a young boy.

INT: What else stands out in your mind about your time in Cuba before we start with the United States? Any other memories you want to share?

RR: No. [LAUGHING] A very minor thing-- we lived next door to a movie house. And every after-- they would have matinees every afternoon, and they would blare out a lot of music and songs. And that was my first exposure to the English language, which I imagine helped me later on when I had to learn English. I do remember that. And I remember, again, going to synagogue with-- again, it was with my mother, because my father at the time was sick. But it was a relatively pleasant experience.

INT: So now you're coming to the United States. What were your expectations of America?

RR: Well, I was looking forward, you know, to learning English first of all. [LAUGHING] Fortunately, my mother spoke English to begin with, so that made it a little bit easier, because she taught my brother and me, you know, a lot of the English that we ended up learning. She made it a lot easier for us. But-- because we ended up moving to New Jersey, which was where my mother had a distant cousin, who actually made it a lot easier for us.

INT: What were your first impressions of Americans and of the United States?

RR: Modern-- the word modern. [LAUGHING]

INT: How were you treated by the American people?

RR: I was-- I was treated well. Of course, we had some difficulties, because I was a foreigner. And again, I was at the time about 11 years old. And on the street where we lived, some of the boys were a little, more or less, shall we say, like bullies. But there was no permanent problem. We all got things straightened out pretty quickly. But there was the usual. I guess it's a normal-type situation with boys our age. And, of course, I didn't really speak any English yet, so that didn't make things too easy either.

INT: When you came here, it sounds like you had some experience with anti-foreign--

RR: Yes.

INT:

RR: Mm-hmm, yeah.

INT: Did you experience any antisemitism in this country?

RR: Some, somewhat. Nothing is very serious, but, you know, some people, you know, say, there goes the Jew and stuff like that. Because we lived-- when we first moved to New Jersey, we lived in a-- we lived in Camden, New Jersey. And we-- my mother's cousin had a rooming house in what we call-- well, I call it the blue collar section where we lived for quite a while. And the people there weren't quite as friendly towards foreigners, you might say. But we had no serious problems, just the small things.

INT: What did people ask you? And I-- when I mean you, I mean you and your family, not just you as the individual. But what did Americans ask you about your time in Europe or your time in Cuba? Were they curious about that?

RR: Not too much, no no, there wasn't too much curiosity, as far as I can remember. [PAUSES] I still had an accent because I spoke a lot of French, and people called me Frenchie. That was my nickname. But there wasn't-- there weren't too much-- there weren't too much questions. [LAUGHING]

INT: What adjustments were challenging for you or your family?

RR: Mostly language. Again, as I said, my mother spoke English, and she was pretty fluent in it. My brother and I had to learn it, so that was, you know, a real adjustment. And, of course, you know, to make friends and things like that in school. But [PAUSES] it was all doable. [LAUGHING]

INT: Brings me to my next question. How much education had you been able to get by the time you got to New Jersey? And then I'll ask you starting school in New Jersey.

RR: Yeah, I didn't really. You know, we missed school, you know? I had some in Cuba, but then when we first came to New Jersey, then I was enrolled in school and didn't speak English. [LAUGHING] But I still managed. [PAUSES 5 SECONDS]

I do remember that in those days-- I don't think that's the case now, but in those days, my teacher emphasized the fact that we should learn the Pledge of Allegiance and the Lord's Prayer before-- at the beginning of every school day.

INT: You didn't speak English, and you hadn't had a lot of opportunity for education. Did they start you in school in the United States with your age group?

RR: Yes, they did. Yes. And fortunately, at the school where they put us, we had a very understanding principal and some really good teachers.

INT: Were you able to get any Jewish education?

RR: It was funny. By the time my bar mitzvah year came, I hadn't-- had no knowledge of Hebrew or anything like that. But by then, we had moved to a place which was next door to the synagogue. And we had a very understanding cantor there. And he taught me Hebrew, at least to read Hebrew. I never really understood to speak Hebrew. But I was able to read Hebrew, and I was also able to be bar mitzvahed. Because this was when I was in my 13th year.

INT: Had your family maintained the level of religious observance that you had in Europe?

RR: Not really, no. We did belong to a synagogue, which-- and in-- we had originally been what-- I guess what you now call Orthodox. This synagogue was what we now call conservative. And we did belong there, but we weren't regular [PAUSES] attendees, you might say, at services. Although since we lived next door, and if things got tough, and they didn't have a minyan, they would drag me over there because I was bar mitzvahed at the time, and they needed the 10th person for a minyan. So in that sense, we did participate more. And I was also-- I'm trying to think of the right word, the correct word. After the bar mitzvah, I was confirmed also.

INT: While you were starting school in the United States and becoming bar mitzvahed, what was happening with your parents? How were they supporting themselves?

RR: My mother-- my father died within a few months after we came to the United States. And my mother went to work as a bookkeeper. And that's really-- and, of course, she did have some limited funds of her own, but that's pretty much how she supported us.

INT: When did your father die, and what happened to him?

RR: He had that stroke in Cuba. And when we came over in '42, we came over-- I think it was, I said, sometime in the summer. He died in October, and he actually died of pneumonia. In those days, that was still a prevalent cause of death.

INT: What did you and your family, your mother, know about what was going on to the Jews in Europe during the war years? How much did you know?

RR: Well, we were aware of what was happening. And obviously, there wasn't too much general knowledge about the concentration camp situation. But we did we-- we did know, you know, about the problem that the Jewish people had over in Europe. And we were well aware of all that.

INT: Had your family tried to maintain contact with family and friends still in Europe?

RR: Oh, yes. Unfortunately, most of my-- most of our family, which lived in the-- who lived in Latvia and Estonia, they were all wiped out. My mother had twin brothers who were killed, and my father had problem-- had siblings who were killed. My mother had a sister who lived in Sweden, and, of course, she didn't have the problem there.

INT: How much did you, at home or in school, follow the progress of the war-- the battles and whatnot?

RR: Oh, we were well aware of everything that was going on continuously.

INT: What stands out in your mind? When you look back to the war years in the United States, what stands out in your memory?

RR: In the war years, [PAUSES] I became very much involved in a lot of activities, such as in those days you collected papers. You collected tin cans. You did a lot of that kind of stuff. And we were-- I was in the Boy Scouts then. And we were involved in a lot of that kind of situations, you know, until the end of the war, until '45.

INT: Do you remember any deprivation in this country during the war years?

RR: No, the only deprivation you might say was the fact that we had rationing, and there are certain foods that we didn't have. And, like, my cousin, who was basically our mentor, he had a car that-- I forget. He had, I think, an A or B sticker, which meant that he could only get so many gallons of gasoline a month-- you know, those kind of deprivations, certainly nothing that would affect us in terms of our health or well-being or anything like that. It's just a normal war times-- unfortunately, wartime stuff.

INT: Do you remember when Roosevelt died?

RR: I sure do. Yeah. Yeah, I remember that.

INT: Can you share some of your memories on that?

RR: Well, no. All I remember, that there was a guy by the name of Truman who took over, and nobody knew much about him in those days. Because, you know, originally, Roosevelt's vice president was Wallace, and then he got dumped for Truman. And you know, so we were well aware of all that kind of situation.

INT: How would you describe the mood in the country when Roosevelt died? From your perspective as a, you know, a young person?

RR: Basically, it was a big tragedy. Everybody was crying. They all thought that, you know, the greatest man on Earth had died. It was a very, very sad time of-- unfortunately, in view of history, [LAUGHING] Roosevelt wasn't exactly the person that a lot of people thought he was. But that's politics, and I'm not getting into that. But in those days, everybody-- you know, it was-- everybody was in mourning.

INT: Do you remember hearing about Hitler's death?

RR: I remember hearing about it, yeah. And we were well aware of VE Day and VJ Days [LAUGHING] in those days. Was a lot of celebration.

