RR: This was in the-- in the late spring. I-- my mother had sent me off to a farm in New Jersey to spend the summer. So I was with a whole bunch of friends. And-- because I was only 14 years old in those days. But some of them had a car, and we went zooming around in the car, beeping the horn, and celebrating. That's what I remember about VE Day. [LAUGHING]

INT: When did you begin to realize what had happened to the Jews under the Nazis in Europe? When did that news--

RR: It all started, I would guess, around 1944, you know, before the war was over, and then, obviously, after the war was over. When Eisenhower made his famous edict to take pictures of all these things that happened in the concentration camp, we were-- we were all aware-- well aware of all that.

INT: How did that impact you, given your personal history?

RR: We were very upset. [NODDING] Because even I remembered some of our relatives who had gotten killed.

INT: What efforts did your mother make to try and find loved ones who were left behind?

RR: Oh, she wrote letters. She went to agencies.

Even after the war, she eventually ended up going back to Europe. And, of course, that was all for naught, as far as finding anybody. But they were all gone.

INT: No survivors.

RR: No. As a matter of fact, we ended up later on going to Israel and going to the memorial there, where they had all the names of many of the people. And we found some in there.

INT: Were you able to learn how they were deported or murdered?

RR: No.

INT: Did you--

RR: No. We never got any details on any of that.

INT: You mentioned Israel. What do you remember regarding the birth of the state of Israel? Did it have special meaning for you?

RR: I'm sorry. I couldn't hear the end of your sentence.

INT: I asked about the birth of the state of Israel. Do you remember--

RR: Oh, yes.

INT: --the birth of the state? And did it have special meaning for you?

RR: Oh, yeah. I remember listening to the radio when the-- I guess it was the United Nations basically stated that, you know, Israel was a nation. And we had big celebrations about that, you know? I remember that vividly.

INT: Is there anything more you wanted to say about it?

RR: No. You know, as I said, we ended up going there. I've been there a couple of times. And I have always felt that it was one place you could go as a Jew. [NODDING]

INT: What did you think you would be when you grew up? As you were going through traveling, and moving around, and then, you know, spending the war years here, what did you think you would be as an adult?

RR: This is one of these things that maybe it's my stubborn nature. But ever since I was in my early teens, I wanted to be an engineer. And that's what I ended up being.

INT: Can you tell me about your education?

RR: Well, I ended up going to university, gaining a bachelor's. Then I also got a master's in engineering. And then I also got another master's in engineering management. So I was, I guess, a self-styled scholar for quite a while.

INT: Where did you go to school?

RR: I went to school at Drexel University, the University of Pennsylvania, the George Washington University, and also took courses at the University of Alabama. So it was quite a trip. [LAUGHING]

INT: Did you ever serve in the American military?

RR: Yes, I was in the Army.

INT: When was this?

RR: I was in the Army in 19-- from 1957 on.

INT: For how long?

RR: Well, I was actually in the active service only for a year. But I was in the reserves for six years.

INT: And what were your duties and responsibilities when you served?

RR: I was basically in-- I already had my engineering degree, so I just did electronic stuff in the Army. I did not any specific thing. But I have an interesting anecdote. For some reason, after basic training, my records got confused. And so they gave you tests and stuff. And so apparently, I did really well in the tests. And the colonel called me in and said, you know, with your aptitude, you know, you should try to get a high school diploma. And I said, what? He says, yeah, well, you know, it says here that you never even finished high school. I told him, well, I have a master's degree. And [LAUGHING] it turned out that the records had gotten confused. [LAUGHING]

INT: What was your final rank?

RR: My final rank was private. I was glad to get out. [LAUGHING] Military was not for me.

INT: Excuse me, I'm sorry. Did you experience any antisemitism either in your advanced studies or in the military?

RR: In the military, I did experience antisemitism, which I got straightened out very quickly.

INT: Can you tell us about it, please?

RR: Well, I just-- there was this one person, and I literally threatened him, and he backed off. [LAUGHING] But [PAUSES] I have experienced what you might call not exactly antisemitism. But at one time at work, my very superior manager made a comment about well, we have to Jew them down a little bit. And, of course, I let him know what I thought about that.

INT: I assume you are an American citizen.

RR: I sure am.

INT: When did you become an American citizen, and what is it like for you to be an American?

RR: Oh, god. It was-- I think in those days, you had to wait five years. And I was still in high school, so it was approximately in 1947, '48, something like that.

INT: What is it like for you to be an American citizen?

RR: I'm very proud. Even in today's situations, I'm still proud to be an American.

INT: Can you tell us a little bit about your career?

RR: Well, I was an electrical engineer, and I was involved in doing a lot of research work. [PAUSES] I was involved in radar. I was involved in airships, [PAUSES] also underground communications-- a whole raft of things that I was involved in, but mostly design and research activities.

INT: Did you marry and have a family?

RR: I was married and had a daughter. Unfortunately, my wife passed away very young. She was only 36 years old. And five years later, I remarried, and we've been married 44 years.

INT: What did you tell your family about your wartime experiences and about the family that was lost in the war?

RR: Well, I pretty much told them everything. Of course, they were always very curious to begin with, and I never held anything back. I basically told them everything that happened. [PAUSES] And [LAUGHING] when the movie Dunkirk came out a couple of years ago, there was, of course, a lot of discussion about that, because I had told them about my experiences there.

INT: Can you share some of what you talked about with regard to the movie?

RR: There wasn't really much to talk about. You know, the movie was very realistic. It was really pretty much exactly what I witnessed. So [PAUSES] there isn't much else to be said there.

INT: Do you speak publicly, or have you spoken publicly about your experiences?

RR: I think, if I remember correctly, when I was in high school, [PAUSES] there was a public speaking competition, which I'm-- which I entered. And I seem to remember that I spoke about that and those. But other than that, there was never any real occasion for me to do that.

INT: I realize you were young during the Nuremberg trials. Did you follow the trial at all?

RR: Oh, absolutely. Sure, yeah, I was watching it all the time.

INT: What do you remember about that?

RR: Well, you know, I remembered [PAUSES] that some of the defendants got off pretty easily, that many of them got what they deserved. [PAUSES] You know, it was all a very interesting type situation.

INT: What about the Eichmann trial? Did you follow that?

RR: I did in those days. I don't remember too much about it, to tell you the truth. But I did follow it, including the glass cage in which he had to sit.

INT: Did you feel justice was done in Nuremberg or in the Eichmann trial?

RR: In what? Justice was done where?

INT: In Nuremberg or in Jerusalem with the Eichmann trial-- did you feel justice was done?

